{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ 'control_plane.modules'|trans }}{% endblock %}

{#
	**What this deployment charges for each module** (XIV-101).

	Two facts per row, from two different places, and the page is largely about
	not letting a reader confuse them:

	  * *the state* — how far along the module is, platform-wide, decided by
	    whoever builds Xivi (§6.2). A fact about the code.
	  * *the price* — what **this** installation charges its customers for it. A
	    fact about a business, which is why it is a control-plane row and not a
	    field on a blueprint: a price in `packages/invoice/` would be a price every
	    deployment inherits and none of them chose.

	Either one saying no takes the module out of the store, and the two say no for
	entirely different reasons — so both are drawn, in words, in their own column,
	and the *combination* is drawn again as its own column rather than left for the
	reader to work out.

	**Nothing here comes from a tenant.** Every value is a `module` row of the
	control-plane database crossed with the blueprints this build compiled in, so
	this page opens no tenant connection at all — the same property the tenant list
	next door is built around (§8.10), and `ModulePriceTest` asserts it the same
	way.

	**Amounts are drawn as they are stored**, a plain decimal string with the
	currency code beside it, and deliberately not through a locale-aware currency
	formatter. On a customer's screen a formatted price is right; here the value
	being read sits three centimetres from the input that edits it, and a reader
	who sees `1'234.50` above a box containing `1234.50` has to work out which of
	the two the database holds. One form, on the page where somebody is typing.
#}
{% block body %}
	<main class="container page-shell py-4">
		{{ include('@XiviControlPlane/_nav.html.twig', {current: 'control_plane_modules'}) }}

		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		{#
			**The line an operator is meant to read before anything else.**

			A published module nobody has priced is invisible in every customer's
			store, and the reason for that is on this page and nowhere else — the
			customer sees an absence, not a message. So it is said at the top, with
			the module keys in it, and only when there is something to say: a banner
			permanently reading "0 modules need a price" is furniture, and furniture
			is what the eye learns to skip (XIV-58 made the same argument for the
			same shape one page over).

			Warning rather than danger. Nothing is broken and nobody is being charged
			wrongly; a decision has not been made yet, which is exactly what
			`unpriced` means and is a state a deployment may sit in on purpose while
			it works out what to charge.
		#}
		{% if unpriced is not empty %}
			<div class="alert alert-warning d-flex align-items-start gap-2" role="alert">
				<i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<div>
					<strong>{{ 'control_plane.modules_unpriced'|trans({'%count%': unpriced|length}) }}</strong>
					<div class="small font-monospace">
						{{- unpriced|map(m => m.key)|join(', ') -}}
					</div>
					<div class="small">{{ 'control_plane.modules_unpriced_hint'|trans }}</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{#
			**And the other thing that is nobody's mistake and still needs saying.**

			An installation that has never set `PRICE_CURRENCY` prices in nothing,
			so every figure on this page is a bare number. §8.6 refuses to guess a
			currency for a customer because a guessed one is wrong quietly; the same
			holds for a price list, and the honest consequence is that the page has
			to name the variable. It is an environment variable rather than a field
			on this page on purpose — see `App\Registry\Pricing\PriceCurrency`, which
			is also where the argument for why *prices* are emphatically not
			environment variables lives.
		#}
		{% if currency is null %}
			<div class="alert alert-info d-flex align-items-start gap-2" role="alert">
				<i class="bi bi-info-circle" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<div>
					{{ 'control_plane.price_currency_unset'|trans }}
					<code>PRICE_CURRENCY</code>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{% if modules is empty %}
			<div class="card">
				<div class="card-body">
					<h2 class="h6">{{ 'control_plane.no_modules'|trans }}</h2>
					<p class="text-body-secondary mb-0">{{ 'control_plane.no_modules_hint'|trans }}</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% else %}
			<div class="table-responsive">
				<table class="table align-middle">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'common.module'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.module_state'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.module_price'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.module_in_store'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.module_set_price'|trans }}</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						{% for module in modules %}
							<tr>
								<td>
									<div>{{ module.name }}</div>
									<div class="small text-body-secondary font-monospace">{{ module.key }}</div>
									{#
										A decision that outlived its code (§6.2). Worth showing rather
										than hiding: a row naming a module this deploy no longer ships
										is a deploy accident, and this list is where somebody notices.
										Its price is still readable and still editable — the row is
										the platform's memory of a decision, and a build that has lost
										the module has not unmade it.
									#}
									{% if not module.inBuild %}
										<div class="small text-body-secondary fst-italic">
											{{ 'control_plane.module_not_in_build'|trans }}
										</div>
									{% endif %}
								</td>
								<td>
									{# Colour and the word, never colour alone — the tenant list's rule. #}
									<span class="badge text-bg-{{ module.state.isOfferedInStore ? 'success' : 'secondary' }}">
										{{ ('control_plane.module_state_' ~ module.state.value)|trans }}
									</span>
								</td>
								<td>
									{#
										**Four states, drawn as four different things**, because the
										entire point of the ticket is that they do not collapse into
										two. In particular *unpriced* is not a blank cell and not a
										zero: it is a sentence saying nobody has decided, in italics,
										which is the one reading that cannot be mistaken for "free".
									#}
									{% if module.pricing.value == 'priced' %}
										<span class="font-monospace">{{ module.amount }}</span>
										{% if currency %}<span class="text-body-secondary">{{ currency }}</span>{% endif %}
									{% elseif module.pricing.value == 'free' %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-light">{{ 'control_plane.pricing_free'|trans }}</span>
									{% elseif module.pricing.value == 'not_for_sale' %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-secondary">{{ 'control_plane.pricing_not_for_sale'|trans }}</span>
									{% else %}
										<span class="text-body-secondary fst-italic">{{ 'control_plane.pricing_unpriced'|trans }}</span>
									{% endif %}
									{% if module.decidedAt %}
										<div class="small text-body-secondary">
											{{- 'control_plane.module_decided'|trans({'%time%': module.decidedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i')}) -}}
										</div>
									{% endif %}
								</td>
								<td>
									{#
										The two columns to the left, combined — stated rather than left
										to the reader, because the combination has three ways of being
										no and a reader working it out from two badges will get it
										right until the day they are in a hurry.
									#}
									{% if module.offeredInStore %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-success">{{ 'control_plane.module_offered'|trans }}</span>
									{% else %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-secondary">{{ 'control_plane.module_withheld'|trans }}</span>
									{% endif %}
								</td>
								<td>
									{#
										**Radios rather than a select**, which is §6.3's argument
										borrowed for a smaller decision: four options, one of which
										("not for sale") is the one a select would hide behind a
										click, and the whole ticket is about those four being visible
										as four. It is reversible, unlike the preset choice §6.3 was
										arguing about, so nothing here needs a warning above it.

										The amount box is always present rather than revealed by the
										radio, because revealing it needs scripting and this page has
										none: a form that works without JavaScript is what the signup
										page (§8.13) settled for the same reason. It is ignored unless
										"priced" is chosen, and the server is what ignores it — the
										browser is a courtesy here and the controller is the check.
									#}
									<form method="post"
									      action="{{ path('control_plane_module_price', {key: module.key}) }}"
									      class="d-flex flex-wrap align-items-center gap-2">
										<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('module-price') }}">

										<fieldset class="border-0 p-0 m-0">
											<legend class="visually-hidden">
												{{ 'control_plane.module_set_price'|trans }} — {{ module.name }}
											</legend>
											{% for choice in ['unpriced', 'free', 'priced', 'not_for_sale'] %}
												<div class="form-check form-check-inline me-2">
													<input class="form-check-input" type="radio"
													       name="pricing" value="{{ choice }}"
													       id="pricing-{{ module.key }}-{{ choice }}"
													       {{ module.pricing.value == choice ? 'checked' : '' }}>
													<label class="form-check-label small"
													       for="pricing-{{ module.key }}-{{ choice }}">
														{{ ('control_plane.pricing_' ~ choice)|trans }}
													</label>
												</div>
											{% endfor %}
										</fieldset>

										<div class="input-group input-group-sm" style="width: 11rem;">
											<label class="visually-hidden" for="amount-{{ module.key }}">
												{{ 'control_plane.module_amount'|trans }}
											</label>
											{#
												`inputmode="decimal"` and no `type="number"`: the value is
												a decimal string all the way to a NUMERIC column (§5.9),
												and a number input hands back whatever the browser's
												locale made of it. `step` and spinners on a price are the
												wrong affordance anyway.
											#}
											<input class="form-control" type="text" inputmode="decimal"
											       name="amount" id="amount-{{ module.key }}"
											       value="{{ module.amount }}"
											       autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
											       placeholder="0.00">
											{% if currency %}
												<span class="input-group-text">{{ currency }}</span>
											{% endif %}
										</div>

										<button type="submit" class="btn btn-sm btn-primary">
											{{ 'common.save'|trans }}
										</button>
									</form>
								</td>
							</tr>
						{% endfor %}
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
		{% endif %}
	</main>
{% endblock %}
